  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072215",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-91045",
      "knowledge_point_id": "7ff7f935-dcec-5b4c-ba1d-e0b4c9e197a4",
      "knowledge_point": "Applying Differential Operators With Functions of X as Coefficients",
      "problem": "Given that $D={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}-2x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x},$ what is $D(y)$ for $y={x}^{4}?$",
      "worked_solution": "The given operator is a third-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={f}_{3}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y.\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{3}={x}^{2},{f}_{2}=-2x,{f}_{1}=x,$ and ${f}_{0}=0.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D({x}^{4}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}-2x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}){x}^{4} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}({x}^{4})-2x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}({x}^{4})+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{4}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(4{x}^{3})-2x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{3})+x(4{x}^{3}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(12{x}^{2})-2x(12{x}^{2})+4{x}^{4} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(24x)-24{x}^{3}+4{x}^{4} \\\\  & =24{x}^{3}-24{x}^{3}+4{x}^{4} \\\\  & =4{x}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
